how does netflix's vikings valhalla  relate to the original history series is  the same creative team making the sequel  will it be historically accurate keep  watching for all those answers and a lot  more  just when you thought you had a handle  on all the various descendants relatives  and acquaintances of ragnar lothbrok the  story of valhalla jumps more than 100  years into the future according to a  netflix press packet the sequel series  begins in the year 1002 kicking off with  a particular historical event that could  be the viking attack on london bridge  led by olaf haroldson in the trailer but  historically that event didn't take  place until several years later if it  happened at all so it's more likely  something else another candidate is the  saint bryce's day massacre in which king  aethelred ii ordered the slaughter of  all the danish vikings living in english  territory hoping to put an end to years  of viking raids instead king aethelred's  actions provoked the danish king's swain  fork beard to increase the frequency and  severity of his attacks eventually  forcing aethered and his queen emma of  normandy into exile considering many of  the main players in the saint price's  day massacre are confirmed characters  for the first season of vikings valhalla  seems like a strong dramatic contender  to kick off the series the entire north  is threatened  by the english king whatever event marks  the beginning of vikings valhalla  prepare to see a significantly more  complex political landscape than in  vikings in the netflix press package  showrunner jeb stewart explains what the  world will look like the vikings had  been living in the british isles and it  settled into big areas called the  danelaw the dane law had started to grow  not just with danes but also with swedes  and norwegians and folks from all over  scandinavia saxon nobles were afraid  that if you went out another generation  suddenly there wouldn't be any  difference between saxons and vikings  the vikings on vikings spent most of  their time engaged in power struggles  but there was one man in control behind  the camera creator michael hearst wrote  every single episode of vikings so  perhaps it's not surprising that he'll  be less involved in the sequel series  first will still be an executive  producer alongside vikings vets morgan  o'sullivan and showrunner jeff stewart  stewart stuart giumeno is one of the  co-writers of die hard  given the time jump we likely won't be  seeing any of the familiar faces from  vikings unless they appear as ghosts  visions or in flashbacks fortunately the  same person who worked his casting magic  for vikings is back for valhalla frank  boyzell returns as casting director and  has filled the new series with cast  members of sullivan says bring a fresh  youthful energy to this story also back  is viking's historical consultant justin  pollard he will be the one digging into  the obscure history being explored by  the show working to make sure it's as  accurate as possible he spent months  consulting with stewart on the new  series before writing even started as  for the shooting location viking's  valhalla is being shot in ireland just  like the original series so there will  be the same breathtaking scenery fans  expect to see  vikings had one indisputable lead  character ragnar lothbrok vikings  valhalla though will have three central  characters sharing the spotlight  legendary viking explorer leif erikson  pagan shieldmaiden freitas eric's  daughter and the norwegian prince and  viking berserker harold sigurdsson  brother and sister leif and freitas will  meet harold for the first time during  the events of the series stewart says he  chose to build the story around these  three characters because of what they  represent in the netflix press kit he  says for me they each embody different  elements of the viking spirit bringing  leif and freitas who were both  greenlanders to norway to categort and  embedding them in the viking story just  seemed a very natural place to go  centering vikings valhalla around three  characters will also help offer a  broader perspective on the culture and  characters which will be important to  help modern viewers relate to the story  stuart added it's a timeless story in a  strange way if you take massive violence  out of the equation the vikings embody  some of the elements that we still most  want for ourselves our characters are  dealing with relationships and issues  that are just as relevant today as they  were a thousand years ago and those  stories will always excite and captivate  us  while some suspected that the title  vikings valhalla meant a glimpse into  the viking afterlife we now know that  the title of the series isn't  necessarily about that sort of life  after death the title valhalla was  chosen because the show depicts the  events that lead to the end of the  viking age which historically ended in  1066. stewart said we played with a lot  of new titles and kept coming back to  valhalla because the idea of the show is  inherently tied to the end of this era  it's all about showing this incredible  time in history and how all things  eventually come to an end but don't get  too worried just yet about the end of  the vikings era since the series begins  in 1002 the show has more than six  decades to play around with before the  viking age comes to a historical close  and it's not like any period of history  ever came to a neat stop so we wouldn't  worry about running out of viking  legends to dramatize anytime soon  preda gustavsson will be playing freitas  eric's daughter the daughter of eric the  red and sister of leif erikson on  valhalla in an uncanny coincidence  gustav sen's real-life father is also  named eric and her real-life brother is  also named leif and it doesn't end there  gustav's real-life nickname is also  freitas i wonder she really threw  herself into the role reading multiple  books to prepare with her favorite being  valkyrie the women of the viking world  gustavson was also an accomplished  writer prior to the show giving her an  advantage during the scenes on horseback  we can only hope she also did some  strength training too since gustav since  costume is actually the heaviest of all  the lead characters on the show there  are more than 2 700 hand-cut leaves that  decorate her shield maiden armor  a big part of bringing the characters to  life on vikings was the intricate  costumes designed in the show's final  few seasons by costume designer susan  o'connor cave cave is back for vikings  valhalla using her keen eye for detail  to enhance the story for example while  the high neck dresses that emma of  normandy wears on the show are  indicative of her station and royal  bearing they're also meant to act as her  armor they convey that although she  doesn't charge into battle like a shield  maiden she's still a warrior in her own  right similarly although the devout yarl  kora holds no official position in the  church the silhouette of his costume is  meant to evoke a cassock a garment  traditionally worn by certain christian  clergy cave also incorporated the  viking's cultural and religious shifts  into her costume designs for vikings  valhalla you may notice richer colors  and more chainmail in the vikings  costumes meant to reflect their  transition to christianity color and  texture also play a big role in  enhancing the characters with the  greenlanders costumes meant to reflect  their connection to the earth and the  vikings costumes overall appearing more  organic favoring wolves linens and furs  the saxons meanwhile will wear mostly  velvets silks and brocades with heavy  embellishments highlighting the stark  differences between the cultures  while vikings developed a reputation for  playing a bit fast and loose with  history the truth is a bit more  complicated than that justin pollard  served as historical consultant on both  vikings and vikings valhalla making him  responsible for all the research that  helped inform the story lines but  researching viking history isn't as  simple as just referencing old texts as  jeb stewart explained in his netflix  interview there are a million different  ways to look at history we don't know a  whole lot about the original vikings  they didn't have a written language so  the documentation is tricky they left us  the sagas but the sagas were written 200  years after the end of the story and  they were written by christians we know  archaeologically what has been dug up  but we're still discovering new things  about viking culture obviously it's  tricky to make an authentic show about a  group of people whose history was only  written down centuries after the fact  and even then only by people outside  their culture there's no simple correct  version to defer to which means that  many creative decisions are based on  historical interpretations rather than  historical facts still the team behind  vikings valhalla did their best to be as  true to history as they could steward  says ultimately we've made an authentic  show sometimes we've moved dates or  characters around a bit but the  historical pieces are all there  while things did begin to shift by the  end of the series the original vikings  mostly pitted the pagan vikings against  the english christians with only  isolated characters like athelstan  expressing any interest in bridging that  gap however the series does feature the  conversion of the viking warrior rolo to  christianity at first in name only but  later on in practice a hundred years  later the religious landscape in europe  looked very different with many vikings  having followed in rollo's footsteps and  converted to christianity vikings  valhalla will explore the conflict  between the pagan and christian vikings  with both sides being well represented  by the viking families in the series of  its three main characters freitas and  leif will both still worship odin and  the old gods while harold is a christian  more broadly while the greenlander  vikings will still be pagan the  norwegian and danish vikings will have  largely converted to christianity  according to stewart the two groups will  clash in vikings valhalla in a religious  civil war christians are my true enemy  and you are mine he said in the netflix  press kit it's a long and somewhat  complex timeline but when our story is  set christian vikings were mostly very  sincere very tough and very focused on  absolute and total conversion to  christianity or violently wiping  paganism off the map entirely  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite shows are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]

